<!--Post: 2403--><!--Time: 1103603216-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2403"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 20, 2004 - 8:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->My wife spoke today to an old acquainence in Kansas and was told this interesting story.  The tale is that of a &#39;friend of a friend&#39; but specific names were used and my wife believes the person relating the story to be accurate.  I do not mention any names at the request of my wife. <BR>A young woman living in apartment in Wichita had a  frightening event occur within the past month:  She returned home to her apartment, where she lived alone.  The door was locked and nothing was amiss, she is not aware of any forced entry or items missing.  What she did find was someone had left a classic hangman&#39;s noose tied from rope on her kitchen table.  She moved out the next day. <BR>I do not have any other information as to the part of town or if she had any recent breakups or other personal conflicts which could have motivated this.  I would put this down to another of those stories except for my wife vouching for the reliability.  The person who told my wife heard the story directly from the mother of the young woman in question, relating they helped the daughter move out immediately. <BR>When I first heard it this did not sound like BTK to me, but the more I thought, rope, specific knots, and entering a home of a single female when nobody was there has enough in common to make me wonder.   <BR>Before I am inundated with questions, I have told all I know in this post. Any other strange tales recently in Wichita?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2403-->
<!--Post: 2420--><!--Time: 1103636449-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2420"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:dammitslicky@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cougar<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cougar<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 21, 2004 - 5:40 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Yes. And reading your post I am mad with myself for not reporting this when it happened. After I post here I intend to email coldcase with specifics as to my residence so it can be checked out - if they will bother with it at all.  <BR> <BR>I live in Riverside. My back yard is right along the river. We have a dock, as do my neighbors. One morning last spring I took my small children to the dock to feed the ducks and geese. I immediately noticed something hanging from a low tree limb, about 4 feet from the rivers edge. From where I stood it looked to be a child&#39;s skeleton hanging by it&#39;s neck. I never thought it could be real...it looked to me to be a paper mache&#39;. It was downright wicked/spooky looking. My four year old noticed it too and it frightened her. We didn&#39;t stay on the dock for long as it really gave me the creeps. I couldn&#39;t understand why someone would put something like that there and for some dumb reason, didn&#39;t look closer. I mentioned it to my husband and asked him to talk to my neighbor about it - but he never did. I stopped taking the kids to the dock for a week or so. When I did go back alone I saw that the &#39;thing&#39; had fallen from the tree and was on the ground. Due to the water&#39;s edge and the sudden drop off of ground, and a fence between my neighbor&#39;s property and mine, it was close to impossible to access this thing without getting in the water or going through my neighbors side gate - and since he has mean dogs there was no way I was going to do that. It bothered me for a very long time and then little by little I just let it go from my thoughts.  <BR> <BR>It is worth mentioning that our neighborhood is immediately off of 13th street. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2420-->
